    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with two high-ceiling anchors leading the offense: **Yordan Alvarez** (81.25) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (80.98). **Yordan Alvarez** benefits from a high-efficiency matchup against Ure\u00f1a, utilizing his elite xwOBA (.454) to capitalize on a favorable SP_xERA of 4.53. Meanwhile, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** remains a top priority due to his scarcity value; he draws a prime matchup against Sugano, with a 14% park boost and a massive 19% SP skill advantage propelling him into elite territory. **Christian Yelich** rounds out our top-tier starters (68.09) as he faces Ginn, taking full advantage of a 13% dynamic platoon edge to remain a locked-in start.\n\nWe are navigating a few tactical hurdles, specifically the daunting presence of Cease, who forces a slight regression for both **Trea Turner** (52.43) and **Kyle Schwarber** (51.64). Despite the tough SP_xERA of 3.13, both players remain in the lineup for their order-of-batting utility and high-floor production. Our most difficult decision was keeping **Chase Meidroth** (47.99) on the bench. While his score is respectable, he was ultimately outperformed by the positional versatility and higher base projections of **Colt Keith** and **Caleb Durbin**, both of whom offer better situational stability in their respective matchups. \n\nRisk management remains a theme today, as we are closely monitoring the lineup stability of **Dillon Dingler** (45.29) and **Kazuma Okamoto** (40.13), both of whom are hovering near our roster floor. **Kazuma Okamoto** faces a significant test against Wheeler, where his projection is dampened by a 19% negative SP skill adjustment. While we have cleared the roster of non-starters like **Rece Hinds** and **Gary Sanchez**, the Zebras will be watching the bullpen usage closely, as any late-game defensive substitutions for **Junior Caminero** or **Lars Nootbaar** could truncate their counting stat opportunities.",
